    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "When I grew up, yeah, when I grew up, which finally is young in the Senate, I don't feel so young, so young in some most days, but when I was growing up, I mean liberals, believed in the First Amendment in free speech. I think what's so amazing to me, and you look at the coverage of this story yesterday from the Washington Post and from the New York Times, essentially their headlines are ruling may prevent government from dispelling misinformation. Here's the deal. The government's job is not to police what we say. But you've got now this whole mainstream media enterprise that's parroting messaging from the current administration. Like Woodward and Bernstein are dead. We're like past all of this idea that the press, ironically, that lives and dies by their protections of the First Amendment. Don't care very much about the First Amendment anymore. And I think that's really troubling. So yeah, I'm for look. People say, that things about me all the time. That's part of the deal, right? When you run for, that's part of the deal. I can handle it and you combat that with, well, actually you're wrong because and here's why we should persuade people to do this. I don't think conservatives at all are afraid of these debates. What you see now from the modern left is that narrowing of the bandwidth of what's acceptable because they know if they control language and they control speech that's power and control, right? That's power. And I think that's why there's this real pushback against political correctness in general. There's a real pushback now in this kind of censorship enterprise. And so, like I said, this is a very important fight and I'm in it.",
